ID: B3-Ch09c-Teaming
TEXT:

 "Teaming up with the king or Peter is a bad idea," I say. "They are going to be weaker here than on the earthen plane. We will spend the whole fight keeping them alive. We are better off on our own."

"If you say so, Barry." Heat ripples off Flower's skin. "I am jumping us straight to the Overseer, then. Ready?"

"Ready."

White light folds over us, humming against my teeth. When the glare clears, we are drifting in the blinding expanse of the ether. Twenty feet ahead, the green-eyed drone hovers in place, silent and still.

"Processing," the Overseer drones. "The Overseer protocol thanks you for reducing search parameters. State the purpose of your presence so optimal actions may be calculated."

"Aren't you supposed to be a genius machine?" I say. "Work it out."

"From this response, the Overseer protocol deduces that your stance remains unchanged and you refuse cooperation against the insurgent faction. Is this accurate?"

"Here is your answer."

I drop into stance, cutting my hands through the light to trace the particle paths, drawing the charge into my fingertips. Before the drone finishes adjusting its angle, I snap my index finger forward. Lightning cracks across the void, arcing straight for the lens.

The bolt strikes a pocket of dense air inches short of the metal casing, scattering into blue sparks along a pre-cast shield.

Flower launches three fireballs in a tight cluster. The explosions bloom across the drone, churning smoke through the ether. As the flames thin, a dome of compressed stone around the machine shatters into falling grit.

"Warning," the Overseer says, completely level. "The Overseer protocol will now initialize experimental procedures developed exclusively for the ethereal plane. These procedures derive from Magium data and remain largely untested in combat scenarios. Inquiries and complaints may be directed to section 5C-E789 of the operational codex."

"Experimental procedures?" My stomach drops. "What are you—"

A wave of pitch-black energy erupts from the drone. It hits before the sound can travel.

There is no pain, only the instant unraveling of my conjured body, stripped down to raw thought. The ether goes dark, and my consciousness tears away into nothing.

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Teleport
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch9_conclusions == 2) {
 "All right," I say. "Peter first. Can you jump us there now?"

"On it!" Heat pulses from Flower's hands, and blinding light wraps around us.

The hum fades, leaving us suspended in the open glare of the ether. Ten paces away, Peter and the king of the underground hover together. The king has already formed an ethereal body of his own, solid and dense. Peter's eyes are dull compared to Flower's blazing red irises—he hasn't undergone the evolution that grants her long-range soul-sense and unrestricted teleportation.

"What are you doing here?" Peter demands, his guard instantly up.

"We are heading for the Overseer," I say. "Figured you might want in. What do you say?"

"You expect us to march with you after you announced you and the Overseer were setting an ambush?" Peter sneers. "What kind of fools do you take us for?"

"Come on, Peter. Think for two seconds. That was a joke. Who announces a real ambush in advance?"

"You call it a joke," the king says, his voice low and measured, "yet we know you spoke with the Overseer through the blue crystals while you were underground. Would you care to explain why you held secret council with our enemy on the eve of battle?"

He knows about the crystal transmissions? A rebel scout must have caught us in the corridors.

"Look, the Overseer made an offer," I say. "We spent half an afternoon weighing it, and in the end we turned him down. Think about it. If this were a trap, we would have linked up with the Overseer first and jumped you together. Why come here alone and risk you attacking on sight?"

"What I hear," the king says, "is that you stood on the edge of betrayal and stepped back on a whim. What guarantees you will not change your mind mid-fight?"

"A whim?" I stare at him. "You put a hit on us! How is that not reason enough to consider our options? We heard the Overseer's recording. You told Albert to handle us 'discreetly' if we opposed your coronation. That is open conspiracy to commit murder."

The king pauses, rubbing his chin. "Ah. Now the picture clarifies. If you heard those words, I cannot blame your suspicion. In your shoes, I would have reached the exact same conclusion. Explaining the context now is a waste of breath, so I will take the gamble and trust your refusal of the machine's offer. Let us bury the grievance here and focus on the common enemy."

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_conclusions != 2) {
 "All right," I say. "Peter first. Can you jump us there now?"

"On it!" Heat pulses from Flower's hands, and blinding light wraps around us.

The hum fades, leaving us suspended in the open glare of the ether. Ten paces away, Peter and the king of the underground hover together. The king has already formed an ethereal body of his own, solid and dense. Peter's eyes are dull compared to Flower's blazing red irises—he hasn't undergone the evolution that grants her long-range soul-sense and unrestricted teleportation.

"What are you doing here?" Peter asks.

"We are heading for the Overseer," I say. "Figured you might want in. What do you say?"

"We were moving in that direction ourselves," the king says. "A lift would be welcome. Peter's daily teleportation reserves are limited, and we cannot afford to burn them before the fighting starts."

"Done," I say. "We jump right now."

"Hold," the king says, raising a hand. "Charging in blind is reckless. We need a plan."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch09c-Invented, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Invented)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Invented
TEXT:

 "A plan?" I say. "I figured we jump in and improvise. What is there to plan when we don't even know what the Overseer can do in the ether?"

"We cannot predict every variable," the king replies, "but we must understand each other's capabilities. Crossing wires in the middle of combat gets people killed."

"Fair enough."

"Would you mind going first?" The king tilts his head. "I assume there is a particular reason you are the one accompanying our ethereal companion, rather than the more seasoned mages in your company."

"Right. Earthen mages can't cast here unless they are ethereals or beings from the magical plane. Their unlocked channels mean nothing in the ether. I can cast because I know the mechanics by heart. Decades of studying spellcraft without having a drop of magic in my veins finally paid off."

"Fascinating," the king says. "Could you demonstrate? Do your incantations behave identically to earthen casting?"

"Mostly. The difference is that the particle flow and structural theory must be flawless, or the matrix collapses. Watch."

I trace four rapid patterns in the air. A bead of flame flickers to life, followed by a tight coil of water, a localized gust of wind, and a needle of ice. The king watches the elements dissolve back into raw light, his expression thoughtful.

"A broad repertoire," he observes. "Does this make you the ethereal equivalent of a generalist? How deep does your catalog go?"

"Hard to say. I've confirmed a handful, but the rest are untested. I know the theory for hundreds of spells, but theory and execution aren't always identical in this realm. My stat device stayed behind with my body, so I am relying strictly on memorized matrices. I should also be able to run the multi-step dispelling method I developed while apprenticing under Brinhorn. That structure translates easily enough."

Peter's jaw tightens. "Multi-step dispelling? You mean the Brinhorn Technique?"

"That's the one."

"What do you mean, you developed it?" Peter snaps. "Brinhorn invented it! His name is on the treatise!"

"He asked if he could take the name, and I didn't care," I say. "There are thousands of Barrys wandering the continent. A 'Barry Technique' sounds like a tavern trick."

"You are delusional," Peter says, face flushed with outrage. "Brinhorn taking a non-mage as an apprentice? You expect anyone to believe this nonsense?"

"Easy, Peter," the king says. "The claim sounds extraordinary, but the man just cast across four elements without a channel. That earns him some benefit of the doubt. In any case, we have a generalist on either flank. You two should coordinate well."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09c-Linked, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Linked)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Linked
TEXT:

 "With respect, my lord, calling this man a generalist insults every true scholar in history," Peter says, glaring at me. "He is an outright fraud. To speak Brinhorn's name so casually proves how little he understands. Brinhorn challenges Azarius for Sage of the West nearly every cycle! Who is this 'Barry' to invoke him?"

"You could also say Brinhorn loses to Azarius every single cycle and be just as accurate," I say. "Makes the comparison a lot more grounded, don't you think?"

"You insolent—"

"Enough, Peter," the king cuts in. "Personal opinions aside, we fight together. Maintain professional discipline."

Peter grits his teeth. "...Understood, my lord. I will hold my tongue until the enemy is eliminated."

"Good." The king turns back to me. "Peter models his casting after the great duelists, Brinhorn and Azarius included. He has studied their engagements firsthand. That should tell you the shape of his tactical repertoire."

"Sounds straightforward enough," I say.

"Excellent. In that case, Peter, one technical question."

"Sir?"

"You explained that my earthen body uses its aura to bridge the connection to my soul in this realm. Correct?"

"Yes, my lord."

"Then my soul must possess a corresponding aura to complete the circuit."

Peter blinks. "I... haven't considered the theoretical mechanics, but logically, yes."

"Splendid," the king says. "Then my instincts were sound."

He snaps his arms outward, palms flat. A violent kinetic wave crashes outward, shoving Flower, Peter, and me back ten feet across the open ether.

"A touch diminished compared to physical casting," the king says, rotating his wrists, "but aura manipulation functions here as well. Apologies for the delay. Whenever you are ready."

"Let's move," I say. "Flower, take us in."

"Hold on!"

White light flares around us, vibrating against my marrow. When the glare snaps shut, we are floating in a fresh quadrant of the ether. Twenty-four feet away, the green-eyed drone hangs motionless in the light.

"Processing," the Overseer drones. "The Overseer protocol thanks you for reducing search parameters. One operational query remains prior to tactical execution. Specify the reason you transported yourselves alongside the insurgent leadership rather than arriving unaccompanied."

"Aren't you the great calculating machine?" I say. "Figure it out."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09c-Shockwave, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Shockwave)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Shockwave
TEXT:

 "From this response, the Overseer protocol deduces that your stance remains unchanged and you refuse cooperation against the insurgent faction," the drone says. "Is this accurate?"

"Here is your answer."

I drop into stance, snapping my fingers through the required angles and channeling the ethereal charge. A bolt of lightning leaps from my index finger, striking straight for the green lens. It hits an invisible barrier inches short of the frame, dispersing into a web of sparks.

Flower follows up immediately, hammering the drone with a rapid salvo of fireballs. The concussions shake the air. When the smoke clears, a shell of compressed earth encases the machine, already fracturing and shedding stone shards into the void.

"Warning," the Overseer says. "The Overseer protocol will now initialize experimental procedures developed exclusively for the ethereal plane. These procedures derive from Magium data and remain largely untested in combat scenarios. Inquiries and complaints may be directed to section 5C-E789 of the operational codex."

"Experimental procedures?" I ask. "What are you—"

A pulse of pitch-black energy explodes outward from the drone. It tears across the space in a fraction of a second, slamming into a hardened plane of force fifty inches in front of us.

The impact rings like struck iron. The king's invisible aura barrier clouds instantly, white spiderweb fractures spreading across the translucent surface.

"If that blast hits unprotected flesh, it dismantles us instantly," the king says, his jaw set as he sustains the strain. "I can hold against three or four more at most before this shield shatters. After that, reforming it takes considerable time."

"The Overseer protocol apologizes for the inconvenience," the drone buzzes. A second shockwave thuds against the barrier, widening the fractures with a dull crack. The machine's chassis vibrates violently, emitting a grinding hum. "The experimental parameters require recalibration. Please remain patient during system adjustments."

choice("Tell the king to cancel his barrier and to attack the Overseer together with us.", B3-Ch09c-Cancel, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Cancel, v_ac_b3_ch9_consolation = +1)
choice("Tell the king to keep the barrier active a little longer, but to cancel it before it breaks.", B3-Ch09c-Longer, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Longer, v_ac_b3_ch9_consolation = +1)
choice("Tell the king to keep the barrier active until it breaks.", B3-Ch09c-Breaks, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Breaks)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Cancel
TEXT:

 "We can't just sit here waiting for the barrier to break," I say. "We have a better shot if we hit him together."

"If we coordinate an assault, we wait for the next discharge," the king murmurs. "There is a distinct delay between pulses. He requires time to gather the charge."

"Agreed," I say.

"Ready your matrices," the king instructs. "I will drop the ward immediately after."

We prepare our spells and brace. A third shockwave slams the barrier, rattling the fractured plane. The instant the dark energy dissipates, the king drops the shield. We unload everything at once—fire, ice, lightning, and hammering waves of compressed aura driving hard at the drone.

Mid-volley, the drone pulses without the recharge delay. A wave of black force erupts without warning.

The king lunges to reform the barrier, but the speed of the wave beats him. The black tide engulfs us. My conjured body unthreads into particles, sensation vanishes, and consciousness dissolves into the void.

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Longer
TEXT:

 "Keep the ward up a little longer," I keep my voice low. "He isn't finished testing the shockwaves. When the shield reaches its limit, give the signal and we hit him with everything."

"A reasonable compromise," the king replies. "Hold your matrices primed. We release the moment the ward drops."

"Understood." I channel the energy through my fingers, holding the spell on a hair trigger.

Two more black shockwaves hammer the barrier in rapid succession. The surface is nearly opaque with fractures now, groaning under the pressure. Just as I open my mouth to ask how much structural integrity remains, the king barks a command and drops the shield.

We strike simultaneously, unleashing a combined barrage of elements and concussive aura waves at the chassis.

Before our spells can close the distance, the drone discharges again without warning. The black shockwave rips through our incoming volley. The king reaches out to raise another shield, but the wave moves too fast. It washes over us, tearing my ethereal form apart at the seam of thought, and my mind plunges into total dark.

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Breaks
TEXT:

 "This is going to sound counterintuitive," I say quietly, "but we hold the barrier until it breaks."

"Explain," the king says, keeping his focus forward.

"The shockwaves are an unintended glitch in his test," I say. "He said he was trying to resolve the issue. If we let him finish patching whatever is failing, the shockwaves stop."

"You want to let the enemy fix his weapon?" Peter asks.

"I'd rather face whatever the weapon was supposed to be than stay in the blast zone of an unavoidable kill-wave," I reply. "We have zero defense against those pulses once the aura shield fails."

Another shockwave slams the barrier, shaking the ground beneath our ethereal feet. White cracks bite deeper into the translucent wall.

"And when the ward gives out?" Peter asks. "What then?"

"Flower jumps us out of range, and we regroup," I say. "No point dying in an unwinnable grid. At least, I think that's how caution works. I am still adjusting to the concept."

"Fair enough," the king says. "Hold positions until the ward collapses."

We wait. Two more pulses strike the barrier, sending spiderweb fractures racing across every square inch. On the third hit, the ward shatters into vanishing motes of light.

"That's our cue," I say. "Flower, pull us—"

"Thank you for your patience," the Overseer drones, the grinding whine in its chassis cutting out abruptly. "The system fault has been corrected. Operational testing will now proceed as scheduled."

"Just to clarify," I call out, "these scheduled tests don't involve any more omnidirectional disintegration waves, do they?"

"Negative," the drone replies. "The shockwave dispersion was an unintended malfunction."

"Great news," I say. "Nice to know we won't get erased without—"

"Barry, move!" Flower screams.

Heat wrenches my shoulder as she teleports me sideways. A needle-thin beam of pure black energy pierces the exact point where my head had been, searing the light of the ether into an empty void.

"The Overseer protocol records initial beam calibration as a success," the drone announces. "Commencing phase two: concurrent multi-vector targeting. To maintain test integrity, please remain stationary for target acquisition."

A swarm of red-eyed drones materializes across the ether, encircling us in a tightening ring. A black beam cuts past my ribs; I pivot on a burst of Melindra's air currents, throw a lightning arc through two chassis to punch a hole, and bank hard through the wreckage.

"Scatter!" I shout over the hum of the charging lenses. "Split his focus! Keep moving!"

"We know the basics," Peter shouts back, dropping four units with a spread of ice shards before breaking away. "None of us need a lecture!"

Twenty red-eyed drones drop into formation around me, sealing every exit vector.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09c-Researched, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Researched)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Researched
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 0) {
 Before the lenses discharge, I release an expanding ring of fire. The heat slams outward, incinerating the surrounding circle of drones into charred scrap that drifts in the zero-gravity void.

Asking Flower for a broad-area clearing spell was the right call.

That buys a few seconds. My mind works through the problem at speed. Disintegration beams. Pitch-black rays that drink the light around them. Can they be dispelled? No—pure annihilation energy cannot be unraveled like a structured matrix. The old research papers from the western academies concluded that true disintegration only stabilizes under theoretical, ideal conditions impossible on the material plane.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 1) {
 Before the lenses discharge, I throw a dense sphere of water around myself and detonate it into a pressurized burst of water needles. The jets punch straight through the red sensor eyes, leaving twenty dead metal hulls floating in place. The sphere evaporates into mist, clearing my line of sight.

Asking Illuna for a multi-target water matrix was the right call.

That buys a few seconds. My mind works through the problem at speed. Disintegration beams. Pitch-black rays that drink the light around them. Can they be dispelled? No—pure annihilation energy cannot be unraveled like a structured matrix. The old research papers from the western academies concluded that true disintegration only stabilizes under theoretical, ideal conditions impossible on the material plane.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 2) {
 Before the lenses discharge, I whip my arms in an outward arc, releasing a radial storm of wind blades. The razor-sharp currents sever the red lenses and shear through the casings, leaving twenty dead chassis drifting in the void.

Asking Melindra for an omnidirectional wind matrix was the right call.

That buys a few seconds. My mind works through the problem at speed. Disintegration beams. Pitch-black rays that drink the light around them. Can they be dispelled? No—pure annihilation energy cannot be unraveled like a structured matrix. The old research papers from the western academies concluded that true disintegration only stabilizes under theoretical, ideal conditions impossible on the material plane.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 3) {
 Before the lenses discharge, I drive my palms outward, releasing a flash-freeze wave in every direction. Frost encrusts the chassis, locking the thrusters and shattering the lenses into frosted scrap that hangs motionless in the light.

Asking Kate for a wide-radius freeze spell was the right call.

That buys a few seconds. My mind works through the problem at speed. Disintegration beams. Pitch-black rays that drink the light around them. Can they be dispelled? No—pure annihilation energy cannot be unraveled like a structured matrix. The old research papers from the western academies concluded that true disintegration only stabilizes under theoretical, ideal conditions impossible on the material plane.

}
 What was the counter-theory? Every dissertation had footnotes. There was an edge case—an interaction.

Across the field, Peter maneuvers against three flanking drones. Instead of simply blasting them, he times their firing cycles, positioning a floating orb of flame directly into the path of an incoming black ray while side-stepping the line of fire.

The memory hits me. Krygell's treatise on high-density ether reactions.

"Peter!" I yell, banking hard as a black beam singes my sleeve. "You cannot insert raw fire from the outside! Krygell's theorem—the outer perimeter of the beam destroys the flame before it reaches the core. You have to insulate the fire until it sits entirely inside the beam!"

Peter stares across the light, nearly clipping another drone. "You... read Professor Krygell's thesis?"

"Not the point!" I duck under another black streak. "We can make it work, but we need four-way coordination."

"Explain!" Peter barks, shattering two more units with an ice spike as three others drop in behind him.

"Living auras resist disintegration better than raw elements," I shout. "The king's hardened barrier proved that. Flower is an ethereal fire specialist—her flame control is absolute. If the king insulates her fireball with a compressed aura shell, places it in the beam's center, and drops the shell, the flame absorbs the black energy from the inside out. You and I keep the drones off their backs!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09c-Feasible, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Feasible)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Feasible
TEXT:

 "Peter! Is the man's theorem sound?" the king calls out, crushing three chassis with an expanding aura hammer.

"It... it holds, my lord!" Peter shouts back.

"Then we execute," the king says. "Flower, you understand the assignment?"

"Make a fireball and put it inside the black beam," Flower says. "I can do that!"

"Form the core," the king orders. "I will wrap it in hardened aura the moment it ignites."

"Ready!" Flower's hands blaze with orange heat.

"Cover her flanks!" I shout to Peter, wheeling around to intercept four approaching drones with lightning arcs. "She cannot break focus!"

Peter and I hold the perimeter, blasting incoming drones out of the air. Flower thrusts her sphere of flame into the line of fire. The king's translucent aura instantly seals around it, a glowing envelope. A red-eyed drone fires its black ray. The beam swallows the shielded orb whole.

The king snaps his fingers, releasing the ward.

The trapped fire drinks the black energy from the core outward, devouring the beam. The orange light dies, replaced by a churning sphere of absolute black flame that warps the ether around it.

"Black fire..." Peter whispers, eyes wide. "The theorem holds."

"Flower!" I yell. "Put that fire into the green drone!"

Flower whips her arms forward. The black orb shoots across the void. The green-eyed drone snaps five red-eyed escort units into a defensive line to intercept. The black flame punches through them without slowing, erasing the metal chassis on contact.

"Warning. Warning. Warning," the Overseer's drone drones, banking erratically as the black fire tracks its trajectory. "Threat parameters exceed operational tolerances. Procedure one terminated. Initializing procedure two ahead of projected timeline."

"Procedure two?" I shout. "What procedure—"

The black flame snaps back to standard orange and dissipates. Every red-eyed drone vanishes from the ether.

Beneath our feet, a massive blue summoning glyph ignites, blazing across the sea of light.

"No," I mutter, pulling up on air currents to clear the circle. "Not another mammoth. Anything but a mammoth."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09c-Materialize, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Materialize)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Materialize
TEXT:

 The light hardens into a forty-foot nightmare.

It hangs in the ether on broad, leathery drake wings, but it has no hind legs. From a reddish-scaled torso sprout seven serpent necks, each clad in distinct, brilliant scales: crimson, emerald, amber, navy, pale blue, white, and earthen brown.

I have combed through bestiaries from three continents. Nothing like this has ever been cataloged.

"Experiment two," the Overseer announces. "You observe a biological configuration that possessed theoretical viability on the earthen plane but failed to manifest under natural selection. It lacks formal taxonomy. Name it as you see fit. Field observations regarding its combat efficiency will be gathered upon your defeat."

"How about you take your field notes and shove them down your—"

The crimson head snaps forward, vomiting a torrent of flame. I dive on air currents as the heat roars past. A heartbeat later, the pale-blue head unleashes a concentrated beam of frost that sends Flower darting backward on fire jets.

Seven heads. Seven distinct elements—fire, ice, water, air, acid, lightning, and earth.

"Each head carries a specific element!" I shout, circling wide. "Match the colors to the element!"

"Navy is water, white is air, green is acid," Peter calls out, tracking the beast's movements. "Yellow is..."

"Lightning," I say. "Red is fire, pale blue is ice. That leaves brown for earth."

"An earth-breathing serpent?" Peter scoffs. "Absurd."

"Doesn't matter if it spits boulders or shakes the air," I yell back. "It follows the element. Traditional hydra weaknesses apply: burn the tissue, pierce the heart, or take off every head simultaneously. We pick one tactic and commit."

choice("Burn it with fire.", B3-Ch09c-Frantically, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Frantically, v_b3_ch9_hydra = 1)
choice("Stab its heart.", B3-Ch09c-Frantically, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Frantically, v_b3_ch9_hydra = 2)
choice("Chop all its heads off at once.", B3-Ch09c-Chop, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Chop, v_ac_b3_ch9_consolation = +1) if (v_b3_ch9_element != 0)
choice("Chop all its heads off at once.", B3-Ch09c-Frantically, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Frantically, v_b3_ch9_hydra = 0) if (v_b3_ch9_element == 0)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Chop
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 2) {
 "I am taking the heads off," I say.

"Wait," Peter says. "Severing a hydra's head doubles it!"

"Tavern myth," I say. "We fought a subterranean specimen in the ruins. It doesn't double—it just regenerates so fast that partial cuts are useless. You have to take all the heads in a single sweep. Watch."

I whip both arms forward, releasing twenty concentrated wind blades in a clean horizontal plane that shears through all seven necks at once.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 1) {
 "I am taking the heads off," I say.

"Wait," Peter says. "Severing a hydra's head doubles it!"

"Tavern myth," I say. "We fought a subterranean specimen in the ruins. It doesn't double—it just regenerates so fast that partial cuts are useless. You have to take all the heads in a single sweep. Watch."

I snap two pressurized water whips across the void, cleanly severing all seven serpent necks in a single cross-slash.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 3) {
 "I am taking the heads off," I say.

"Wait," Peter says. "Severing a hydra's head doubles it!"

"Tavern myth," I say. "We fought a subterranean specimen in the ruins. It doesn't double—it just regenerates so fast that partial cuts are useless. You have to take all the heads in a single sweep. Watch."

I form a massive, spinning disc of dense ice beside the beast and fling it horizontally, slicing through all seven necks in one smooth stroke.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 1 || v_b3_ch9_element == 2 || v_b3_ch9_element == 3) {
 The seven heads drift away into the light, necks spurting translucent fluid. "Clean cut," I call out. "Down."

The stumps do not seal. They bubble violently, split down the center, and sprout two new necks for every severed trunk. Fourteen heads snap into focus.

Before I can draw breath, the beast folds space, teleporting directly into my face. Fourteen maws strike at once.

Emerald jaws clamp around my torso, flooding my ethereal senses with burning acid. The world dissolves in corrosive fire, and darkness takes me.

}
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Frantically
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch9_hydra == 0) {
 "I'd rather cut all of its heads off," I say.

"Wait," Peter says. "Severing a hydra's head doubles it!"

"Tavern myth," I say. "We fought a subterranean specimen in the ruins. It doesn't double—it just regenerates so fast that partial cuts are useless. You have to take all the heads in a single sweep."

I pause.

"The problem is, I don't have a wide-radius slicing spell memorized. Most of my offensive matrix is fire from Flower, and fire doesn't sever necks cleanly. Peter, can you take the heads?"

"If you think I will risk decapitating a hydra based on your tavern stories, you are out of your mind," Peter says. "Fire is the textbook counter. I am casting a fireball. Follow my lead."

Peter hurls a fireball at the beast. Flower slams the hydra in a roaring pillar of flame, and I follow with one of the heavier fire matrices Flower taught me. The hydra howls in agony through several throats, then folds space, teleporting inches from Flower. Two serpent maws snap at her. She clears the strike with a burst from her fire jets, but the beast banks on massive wings, closing the gap instantly.

"The Overseer protocol omitted that this entity represents the apex of hypothetical hydra evolution," the drone announces. "It possesses elevated heat resistance, multiple internal hearts, and upon total decapitation, each severed neck doubles. Demonstration follows."

A spinning disc of force streaks out of nowhere, slicing cleanly through all seven necks. The blade dissolves. The stumps squirm for a second, then split down the middle, erupting into fourteen distinct heads.

"Ah, damn it," I say. Fourteen heads whip around in a frenzy, searching for targets.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 0 && v_b3_ch9_hydra == 1) {
 "Fire is our safest bet," I say.

"Agreed," Peter says. "Everyone with fire capability, on my mark."

Peter throws a fireball. Flower slams the hydra in a roaring pillar of flame, and I follow with one of the heavier fire matrices Flower taught me. The hydra howls in agony through several throats, then folds space, teleporting inches from Flower. Two serpent maws snap at her. She clears the strike with a burst from her fire jets, but the beast banks on massive wings, closing the gap instantly.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_hydra == 1 && v_b3_ch9_element != 0) {
 "Fire is our safest bet," I say.

"Agreed," Peter says. "Everyone with fire capability, on my mark."

Peter throws a fireball. Flower slams the hydra in a roaring pillar of flame, and I pitch in a standard fireball of my own for good measure. The hydra howls in agony through several throats, then folds space, teleporting inches from Flower. Two serpent maws snap at her. She clears the strike with a burst from her fire jets, but the beast banks on massive wings, closing the gap instantly.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 0 && v_b3_ch9_hydra == 2) {
 "Go for the heart," I say.

"Which one?" Peter snaps. "Every hydra develops a unique internal layout to guard its primary core. Searching blindly wastes time. Incinerate the entire mass and the heart burns with it."

"Fair point," I say. "On three?"

"On my cast," Peter says. "Follow through."

Peter throws a fireball. Flower slams the hydra in a roaring pillar of flame, and I follow with one of the heavier fire matrices Flower taught me. The hydra howls in agony through several throats, then folds space, teleporting inches from Flower. Two serpent maws snap at her. She clears the strike with a burst from her fire jets, but the beast banks on massive wings, closing the gap instantly.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_hydra == 2 && v_b3_ch9_element != 0) {
 "Go for the heart," I say.

"Which one?" Peter snaps. "Every hydra develops a unique internal layout to guard its primary core. Searching blindly wastes time. Incinerate the entire mass and the heart burns with it."

"Fair point," I say. "On three?"

"On my cast," Peter says. "Follow through."

Peter throws a fireball. Flower slams the hydra in a roaring pillar of flame, and I pitch in a standard fireball of my own for good measure. The hydra howls in agony through several throats, then folds space, teleporting inches from Flower. Two serpent maws snap at her. She clears the strike with a burst from her fire jets, but the beast banks on massive wings, closing the gap instantly.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_hydra == 1 || v_b3_ch9_hydra == 2) {
 "The Overseer protocol omitted that this entity represents the apex of hypothetical hydra evolution," the drone announces. "It possesses elevated heat resistance, multiple internal hearts, and upon total decapitation, each severed neck doubles. Demonstration follows."

A spinning disc of force streaks out of nowhere, slicing cleanly through all seven necks. The blade dissolves. The stumps squirm for a second, then split down the middle, erupting into fourteen distinct heads.

"Ah, damn it," I say. Fourteen heads whip around in a frenzy, searching for targets.

}
 A nauseating pull seizes my mind.

It is like being yanked back and forth through a narrow tunnel, neither end letting go. For a fraction of a second, rain hammers my face and cobblestones scrape my cheek in an Ollendor puddle. The next instant, I am floating in the sea of ethereal light.

The world flips between storm and ether half a dozen times. The light around me presses directly against my skull, and a voice echoes inside my thoughts, cold and resonant:

'This is not the arena for your quarrel. Return to your sanctuaries. Disturb these grounds no longer.'

The king freezes mid-cast. "Peter, did you hear that? Was that the ether itself speaking?"

"I... I am not certain," Peter says, his eyes darting across the empty brilliance.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09c-Conducted, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Conducted, v_b3_ch3_stats_unavailable = 0)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Conducted
TEXT:

 "Processing," the Overseer drones. "Insufficient data points. The Overseer protocol requires further contact with the unregistered entity to calculate threat metrics. Experiments proceed on schedule until conclusions finalize."

"Are you insane?" Peter yells. "You want to pick a fight with the ether because your ledgers are incomplete?"

"Remain calm," the drone replies. "Test validity degrades under emotional distress."

Twelve of the hydra's fourteen heads roar in discordant fury. The two earthen-brown heads remain dead silent, their slitted eyes tracking our movements with heavy, predatory focus.

"Fire failed," I say, snapping my fingers through a lightning pattern. "Let's see how it handles current."

A jagged bolt of lightning rips from my hand, striking the beast's chest. The scales scorch and the heads thrash in pain, but the damage seals in seconds. All fourteen necks pivot toward me.

I anticipate the jump, throwing myself backward on air currents as the beast folds space. It appears where I was hovering, both green heads vomiting thick torrents of acid across my trajectory. I throw up a curved slab of stone to take the brunt; corrosive fluid hisses against the rock, chewing through the barrier faster than I can channel structural reinforcement.

"Step back!" the king barks.

A massive sheet of hardened aura slams down between me and the spray, deflecting the corrosive stream into the void.

"Go!"

"Already gone!" I bank hard, putting thirty yards between myself and the maws.

As I pull away, reality fractures again.

This time, the two planes do not trade places—they superimpose. The ether's blinding glare and the storm-lashed plaza of Ollendor exist in my vision at the exact same moment.

The last time my consciousness split like this was inside the seredium tower, when the Magium nearly scrubbed Leila and me from existence. My core awareness anchors in the ether, but I can feel my physical limbs lying in the street below, responding to my will like a heavy puppet hitched to strings.

Flower described this once. How her physical frame feels like a marionette guided from the soul. This is how she lives every waking second.

In Ollendor, red-eyed drones descend on the square. I force my earthen limbs to push up from the rain-soaked cobblestones. Across the plaza, the king's physical body rises smoothly into the air, aura already flaring to meet the swarm.

"You feel both?" I call out on the earthen plane.

"Yes," the king replies simply, ascending toward the clouds.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09c-Ponders, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Ponders)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Ponders
TEXT:

 "What is happening?" Flower's physical form calls out, her voice crisp with Illuna's dry cadence. "How are you operating down here? Flower says the engagement in the ether is ongoing."

Blue flames burn behind her pupils. Illuna holds the wheel.

"The ether itself is intervening," I say, steadying my earthen legs. "A voice ordered us back to our sanctuaries, but the Overseer refuses to break off."

"Sanctuaries meaning your minds in the ethereal plane," Illuna translates, eyes narrowing. "Did it give an ultimatum? How long before the plane unravels your souls?"

"Unravels?" I say. "Nobody mentioned unravelling. We are having a minor dispute with the environment."

"Right," Illuna says. "Settle your dispute quickly before the environment executes you."

A thunderous crack shakes the sky above Ollendor. The king's physical form releases an expanding aura pulse, knocking two dozen drones out of the air in a shower of sparking debris.

In the ether, the green-eyed drone pivots toward the king's soul. "Refrain from dismantling security units. You obstruct standard operational protocol."

"Which protocol is that?" the king asks coldly.

"The transport of incapacitated combatants to secure holding cells for indefinite detention."

"As expected," the king says.

"Failure to cease physical resistance will compel the implementation of severe containment protocols," the Overseer warns. "The long-term prosperity of Ollendor supersedes localized damage constraints."

"Severe protocols?" The king straightens. "Display them."

"Acknowledged," the Overseer says.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09c-Ensure, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Ensure)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Ensure
TEXT:

 Siren horns wail across Ollendor, cutting through the thunder and driving rain.

"A state of emergency is declared for the municipal sector," metallic voices blare from amplification horns across the rooftops. "Evacuate residential dwellings immediately. Security units will guide civilians to designated shelters. This is not a drill."

"That is your master stroke?" I demand of the drone in the ether. "Clear the streets so you can drop heavier ordinance? The king is trashing your scouts as fast as they drop."

The Overseer offers no reply. The hydra surges forward in the light, forcing Peter and me to circle wide.

Simultaneously, heavy tremors shudder through the cobblestones in Ollendor. Through the rain, five towering metal automatons—the gate sentinels—advance through the outer streets, their massive strides crushing rooftops and tearing through storefronts.

"Remain orderly," the loudspeaker drones broadcast above the screaming crowds. "Civilian safety is guaranteed under unit supervision. Structural losses will be reimbursed with interest following pacification. Have a productive day."

"You call this order?" I shout at the machine in the ether. "Leveling a quarter of the city to hunt down three dissidents?"

"The threat coefficient of the insurgent leader exceeds the valuation of municipal infrastructure," the Overseer responds evenly. "Reconstruction will proceed under optimal civic planning post-conflict."

"King!" I yell across both planes. "We have to pull the fight away from the square, or those golems will crush your wounded rebels!"

"I cannot leave the perimeter," the king shouts back from the sky. "The moment I vacate the plaza, the drones take every unconscious soldier on the ground."

"Damn it." He is right.

I turn toward Flower in the ether. "Flower, can you jump back to your mind and pull Hadrik into his sanctuary? He should remember where his mind is by now. If we get the dwarf awake in Ollendor, he can handle the golems."

"Just Hadrik?" Flower asks, floating beside me. "What about everyone else?"

choice("Tell Flower to teleport the rest of my friends to their own minds as well.", B3-Ch09c-Preoccupied, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Preoccupied, v_b3_ch9_hadrik = 0)
choice("Tell Flower that she should teleport only Hadrik and then come back to us.", B3-Ch09c-Preoccupied, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Preoccupied, v_b3_ch9_hadrik = 1)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Preoccupied
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch9_hadrik == 0) {
 "Pull everyone," I say. "Get the whole company into their minds."

"That takes time," Flower warns. "I have to locate each one."

"We can hold the hydra," I say. "Move."

"Don't die while I am gone," Flower says. "I will be as fast as I can!"

White light flares, and she disappears from the ether.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_hadrik == 1) {
 "Just Hadrik," I say. "He has the muscle for golems. We need your fire here—don't stay away longer than necessary."

"Understood," Flower says. "Try to stay in one piece until I get back!"

White light flares, and she disappears from the ether.

}
 I divide my senses between the two battlefields.

In the ether, the Overseer's focus has thinned. Routing commands to his forces in Ollendor leaves the hydra idling, snapping out sporadic breath attacks that Peter and the king easily evade.

In Ollendor, the situation is grim. One of the gate golems has pulled far ahead of the others, closing on the plaza perimeter. In another thirty seconds, its chest cannon will be in range to sweep the square with liquid fire. If Hadrik doesn't wake up immediately, the wounded are dead.

choice("Attack the Overseer on the ethereal plane, while he's distracted.", B3-Ch09c-Distracted, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Distracted, v_ac_b3_ch9_consolation = +1)
choice("Fly towards the metal golem and keep it distracted until Hadrik wakes up.", B3-Ch09c-Upwards, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Upwards)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Distracted
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 0) {
 I seize the opening and drive a concentrated lance of flame straight at the green-eyed drone.

The hydra teleports into the path, taking the full thermal blast across its chest.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 1) {
 I seize the opening and launch a high-pressure jet of water straight at the green-eyed drone.

The hydra teleports into the path, taking the full hydraulic impact across its chest.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 2) {
 I seize the opening and hurl a dense volley of wind blades straight at the green-eyed drone.

The hydra teleports into the path, taking the cutting currents across its scales.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 3) {
 I seize the opening and cast a freezing beam straight at the green-eyed drone.

The hydra teleports into the path, taking the frost across its torso. The chill frosts its scales, but fails to lock the massive joints.

}
#if(v_aura_hardening == 0) {
 The wounds knit instantly. The beast locks onto me, jumping across the light and hounding my maneuvers with relentless fury, completely monopolizing my focus in the ether.

In Ollendor, the vanguard golem stomps into range. Its iron jaw opens, releasing a column of searing flame that sweeps across my undefended earthen body and burns me to ash.

}
#if(v_aura_hardening > 0) {
 The wounds knit instantly. The beast locks onto me, jumping across the light and hounding my maneuvers with relentless fury, completely monopolizing my focus in the ether.

In Ollendor, the vanguard golem stomps into range. Its iron jaw opens, releasing a column of searing flame that melts straight through my hardened aura and burns me to ash.

}
