ID: B3-Ch09c-Holographic
TEXT:

 Before the automaton can recalibrate, a compact silhouette launches from the rubble. Hadrik drives an aura-hardened fist straight into the golem's temple.

The shockwave cracks like a siege mortar. The fifty-ton sentinel lifts off its feet, sails fifty feet backward through the air, and crashes onto an abandoned rowhouse in a plume of splintered timber and slate. Hadrik lands directly on its chest plate, hammering his knuckles into the neck joint until the internal turbine sputters and dies.

"Back in business!" Hadrik bellows over the wreckage, wiping grease on his trousers. "Miss me?"

"You have no idea," I say, descending through the rain.

"What is the play?" Hadrik cracks his neck. "I smash the other four sentries, or do we have a special assignment?"

"Leave the sentinels," the king calls out, dropping from the cloud layer. "I have a higher-priority target for your talents. Gather close. We cannot allow eavesdroppers."

The king and I touch down on the wet street as Hadrik hops down from the destroyed automaton.

"Right," the dwarf says. "Lay it out."

"Do you recall the core objective of this surface incursion?" The king pulls a brass cylindrical mechanism from his tunic. "The sole reason we committed hundreds of lives against the Overseer's legions?"

"Right, right," Hadrik nods. "The core. Smashing the Overseer's main brain."

"Precisely," the king says, handing him the cylinder. "Doctor Reidzek engineered this locator. Observe."

He thumbs a rune on the casing. A miniature three-dimensional grid of light materializes in the air above the bronze plate, mapping the surrounding blocks in fine lines of blue light. It lacks the colored troop markers of the map Arraka projected back in the ogre fortress, but two glowing beacons anchor the coordinates.

The king notes our expressions. "You seem familiar with holographic illusions."

"Arraka cast a tactical projection for us once," I say.

"The banshee in the relic," the king says. "This device runs a basic navigational matrix tracking the beacon to the core's housing. With the Overseer's attention divided between the plaza and the ethereal plane, his primary vault is stripped of mobile defenses. Hadrik, with your mass and velocity, you can breach the facility, dismantle the internal guard, and crush the core in under five minutes. The war ends the moment that housing shatters."

Hadrik scratches his beard. "Sounds solid, but what about the drones and the remaining sentinels? They could flatten you two in five minutes."

"Leave the perimeter to us," the king says, clapping me on the shoulder. "Barry and I will hold the line. Correct, Barry?"

"Sure," I say. "Why not. What could go wrong?"

"There," the king tells the dwarf. "Proceed. Before the machine recalculates our vectors."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09c-Environments, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Environments)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Environments
TEXT:

 "Back in five," Hadrik says. He turns and accelerates into the rain, vanishing down the boulevard like a catapult shot.

"Now," the king says, turning to me. "Can you stall the remaining four sentinels as effectively as the first, or shall I take the armor?"

"I'll take the sentinels," I say. "Keep the drone swarm off the wounded in the square."

"Good hunting," the king says, taking to the air.

"You too," I reply, banking toward the incoming golems.

As I close the distance in Ollendor, the hydra charges my position in the ether. I am forced to halt my physical flight to dodge the lashing serpent necks in the light.

The sentinels keep advancing on the square. I cannot afford to pause one body while piloting the other. If we are going to survive the next five minutes, I have to run both planes concurrently.

I close my eyes in the ether, holding two distinct geometries in my mind: the infinite glare of the light sea, and the cold rain washing over the slate roofs of Ollendor.

Open both gates at once. Run the flows in parallel.

"What are you doing?" Peter's roar echoes across the ether. "Open your eyes! The beast is on you!"

My eyelids snap open.

The dual awareness locks into place. The hydra's crimson jaws lunge at my ethereal torso directly ahead; in the lower field of my vision, four metal sentinels lumber through the Ollendor rain. A blink shifts the primary focus without breaking the secondary thread.

In the ether, I throw a gust of compressed wind to vault over the snapping jaws.

I blink.

My physical frame rolls right through the downpour, dodging a thermal beam from the nearest sentinel.

In the corner of my ethereal awareness, Peter hits the hydra with a pressurized wave of acid. The flesh dissolves and knits back together in seconds, the beast turning on him. In Ollendor, I tuck into the alley between two advancing golems, baiting them to cross lines of fire. The automatons decline the bait, marching inward to crush me between their massive iron palms.

I blink back to the ether, closing on the hydra's flanks while it hounds Peter. I snap my fingers through a specialized immobilizing matrix and drive the spell straight into the base of its spine.

The fourteen heads lock solid. The wings freeze mid-flap. The creature hangs rigid in the light for ten full seconds before the paralysis fractures.

Ten seconds. A human-grade immobilizing charm held a forty-foot mythical apex for ten seconds.

We found the blind spot.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09c-Pillars, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Pillars)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Pillars
TEXT:

 Peter catches my eye across the light. We don't need words.

Every generalist who has ever cracked an advanced combat manual knows the matrix: Sage Drakesbane's Grand Binding, the ritual matrix he used to ground elder drakes. It requires two synchronized casters to weave the perimeter pillars.

"My lord!" Peter shouts as we begin tracing anchoring points in the ether. "Hold the beast for fifty seconds! We are setting a perimeter!"

"Understood," the king calls back.

He thrusts his arms forward, snapping a massive, translucent aura cube around the hydra. The beast thrashes, claws and fangs skidding uselessly against the hardened kinetic wall.

"Will that suffice?" the king asks.

"Keep it contained if it jumps!" Peter calls out, driving an invisible anchor pillar into the space above.

The hydra folds space, appearing ten yards outside the cage; the king instantly drops a second aura box over its new coordinates. The serpent heads exhale a combined storm of fire, but the flames ricochet off the hardened aura walls, roasting the hydra in its own inferno.

"Aura wards do not yield to heat, beast," the king remarks, maintaining the construct.

#if(v_b3_ch9_hadrik == 1) {
 I place three anchor pillars in the ether, blinking rhythmically to check my footing in Ollendor. The four sentinels are closing their perimeter on the street, leveling masonry to eliminate every blind spot. In another ten seconds, all four firing arcs will overlap on my position.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_hadrik == 0) {
 I place three anchor pillars in the ether, blinking rhythmically to check my footing in Ollendor. The four sentinels are closing their perimeter on the street, leveling masonry to eliminate every blind spot. In another ten seconds, all four firing arcs will overlap on my position.

Through the rain, a flash of silver plate cuts across the square. Daren charges into the alley, broadsword drawn and heavy kite shield raised.

"Barry!" Daren shouts. "Get behind the shield!"

}
choice("Try placing myself between two golems again.", B3-Ch09c-Between, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Between) if (v_b3_ch9_hadrik == 1)
choice("Try finding a spot in which none of the golems can attack me.", B3-Ch09c-Finding, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Finding) if (v_b3_ch9_hadrik == 1)
choice("Take cover behind Daren's shield.", B3-Ch09c-Cover, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Cover, v_b3_ch9_cover = 1) if (v_b3_ch9_hadrik == 0)
choice("Tell Daren that it would be better if we each fought two golems instead.", B3-Ch09c-Fought, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Fought) if (v_b3_ch9_hadrik == 0)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Cover
TEXT:

 "Are you certain that shield holds against four concurrent thermal beams?" I shout, banking down behind him.

"We are about to find out," Daren says, planting his greaves and thrusting the heavy kite shield forward.

A dome of translucent white light snaps around us. The four sentinels lock their firing arcs, four columns of liquid fire slamming into the ward in rapid succession. The barrier rings like a gong, vibrating against our ribs under the heat.

"Are we destroying them," Daren grunts through his teeth, "or stalling?"

"Two minutes," I say. "Just hold them for two minutes."

"What happens in two minutes?"

"We win the war," I say.

I blink, throwing my main focus into the ether. I trace two final anchoring runes in the light, completing the circular boundary around the hydra's cage.

I give Peter the mark.

The six ethereal pillars ignite, casting brilliant green energy beams into the sky, weaving a tight geometric web around the cage. The king drops the aura box. The binding beams converge directly onto the hydra's torso, flooding the scales with immobilizing current.

Twelve heads thrash, roaring in deafening agony.

The two brown serpent heads do not roar. They level their gaze directly at me.

No breath weapons. No roars. The tell clicks.

I break into a sprint across the ether, hands snapping through an ancient acoustic inversion matrix while reciting the dampening syllables as fast as my tongue can move.

The brown jaws unhinge. A subsonic shockwave tears from their throats, a blast of sonic pressure violent enough to liquefy inner ears on contact.

My ward snaps into place a fraction of a second before the blast wave hits. A spherical zone of absolute acoustic silence encases Peter, the king, and me. Outside our dome, the ether ripples like struck glass as the hydra roars soundlessly into the void.

The green binding beams reach peak resonance. The thrashing flesh dulls, gray calcification creeping along the necks until the entire forty-foot monstrosity solidifies into solid granite.

I drop the silence ward.

"That... was supposed to paralyze, not petrify," I say, eyeing the fourteen stone heads. "Though I am not complaining."

Peter stares at me, pale. "...How did you know?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09c-Maintenance, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Maintenance)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Fought
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch9_consolation,+1) if (v_b3_ch9_currents == 0 && v_is_dead == 0 && v_reflexes < 2 || v_b3_ch9_currents == 1 && v_is_dead == 0 && v_reflexes < 4)
set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_b3_ch9_currents == 0 && v_reflexes < 2 || v_b3_ch9_currents == 1 && v_reflexes < 4)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_b3_ch9_currents == 0 && v_reflexes >= 2 || v_b3_ch9_currents == 1 && v_reflexes >= 4)
 

"Four thermal beams will shatter that ward," I tell Daren. "Split them. I'll take the two on the right, you pull the pair on the left."

"Are we eliminating them or running a diversion?" Daren asks, angling toward the left sentinels.

"Diversion," I yell. "Keep them occupied for ninety seconds."

"What happens in ninety seconds?"

"We win the war," I shout.

I slip between the two right-hand sentinels, baiting their line of sight.

I blink, shifting to the ether. I plant the penultimate pillar into the light, monitoring the street below with peripheral awareness.

The golem behind me decides collateral damage is acceptable and fires its thermal beam without warning.

#if(v_b3_ch9_currents == 0 && v_reflexes < 2) {
 The delay in shifting focus to my earthen limbs costs me the fraction of a second I need to clear the arc. The thermal beam engulfs me, burning flesh to ash before I can react.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_currents == 1 && v_reflexes < 4) {
 The delay in shifting focus, combined with the sluggish drag of the heavy currents, seals the outcome. The thermal beam engulfs me, burning flesh to ash before I can clear the blast radius.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_currents == 0 && v_reflexes >= 2 || v_reflexes >= 4) {
 Raw reflex saves me. I snap my earthen frame sideways the instant the heat blooms. The thermal beam cuts through empty air and slams directly into the torso of the second sentinel, melting its internal plating and dropping the fifty-ton machine into a heap of dead iron. The firing automaton lowers its head, servos freezing in mechanical confusion.

The pause buys all the time I need.

I blink back to the ether, plant the final anchor pillar, and signal Peter.

The six ethereal pillars ignite, casting brilliant green energy beams into the sky, weaving a tight geometric web around the cage. The king drops the aura box. The binding beams converge directly onto the hydra's torso, flooding the scales with immobilizing current.

Twelve heads thrash, roaring in deafening agony.

The two brown serpent heads do not roar. They level their gaze directly at me.

No breath weapons. No roars. The tell clicks.

I break into a sprint across the ether, hands snapping through an ancient acoustic inversion matrix while reciting the dampening syllables as fast as my tongue can move.

The brown jaws unhinge. A subsonic shockwave tears from their throats, a blast of sonic pressure violent enough to liquefy inner ears on contact.

My ward snaps into place a fraction of a second before the blast wave hits. A spherical zone of absolute acoustic silence encases Peter, the king, and me. Outside our dome, the ether ripples like struck glass as the hydra roars soundlessly into the void.

The green binding beams reach peak resonance. The thrashing flesh dulls, gray calcification creeping along the necks until the entire forty-foot monstrosity solidifies into solid granite.

I drop the silence ward.

"That... was supposed to paralyze, not petrify," I say, eyeing the fourteen stone heads. "Though I am not complaining."

Peter stares at me, pale. "...How did you know?"

}
