ID: B3-Ch09c-Finding
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch9_consolation,+1) if (v_is_dead == 0 && v_perception < 2)
set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_perception < 2)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_perception >= 2)
#if(v_perception < 2) {
 There has to be a blind spot. A dead angle where I can hold position for twenty seconds to finish the ether matrix.

I climb above the rooftops to scan the perimeter. Two sentinels track my ascent and discharge fire beams; I bank hard to clear them. I keep circling, hunting for an alley out of their firing lines.

The automatons fire again. I dive to evade, unaware the first shots were bait. The second pair of sentinels calculates my descent vector and crosses their beams on my landing zone, incinerating me instantly.

}
#if(v_perception >= 2) {
 There has to be a blind spot. A dead angle where I can hold position for twenty seconds to finish the ether matrix.

I climb above the rooftops to scan the perimeter. Two sentinels track my ascent and discharge fire beams; I bank hard to clear them.

From this altitude, the layout clicks. There is a single safe pocket: dead center between the four machines. Positioned equidistant from both pairs, none of the sentinels can fire their cannons without melting the unit directly opposite, and the distance is too wide for them to close on foot before I finish casting.

I drop straight down into the pocket.

The sentinels begin to track me on the way down, but the moment my boots touch the cobblestones at the central coordinates, all four targeting arrays shut down. The machines halt, servos idling in confusion.

I blink to the ether, anchoring the penultimate pillar. I place the final pillar in the ring and signal Peter.

The six ethereal pillars ignite, casting brilliant green energy beams into the sky, weaving a tight geometric web around the cage. The king drops the aura box. The binding beams converge directly onto the hydra's torso, flooding the scales with immobilizing current.

Twelve heads thrash, roaring in deafening agony.

The two brown serpent heads do not roar. They level their gaze directly at me.

No breath weapons. No roars. The tell clicks.

I break into a sprint across the ether, hands snapping through an ancient acoustic inversion matrix while reciting the dampening syllables as fast as my tongue can move.

The brown jaws unhinge. A subsonic shockwave tears from their throats, a blast of sonic pressure violent enough to liquefy inner ears on contact.

My ward snaps into place a fraction of a second before the blast wave hits. A spherical zone of absolute acoustic silence encases Peter, the king, and me. Outside our dome, the ether ripples like struck glass as the hydra roars soundlessly into the void.

The green binding beams reach peak resonance. The thrashing flesh dulls, gray calcification creeping along the necks until the entire forty-foot monstrosity solidifies into solid granite.

I drop the silence ward.

"That... was supposed to paralyze, not petrify," I say, eyeing the fourteen stone heads. "Though I am not complaining."

Peter stares at me, pale. "...How did you know?"

}
