ID: B3-Ch09c-Maintenance
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch9_trinkets,1) if (v_b3_ch9_cover == 1 && v_b3_ch9_currents == 0)
achievement("No need for trinkets",v_ac_b3_ch9_trinkets)
 "Know what?" I ask.

"The acoustic strike," Peter says, still staring at the petrified heads. "How did you deduce the frequency?"

"Process of elimination," I say. "Ever since you pointed out that an earth-breathing serpent made no sense, I kept an eye on the brown heads. They never cast an elemental breath through the entire engagement. More importantly, they never roared when the other twelve heads were howling. If they possessed no elemental affinity and held their throats shut, they were holding a single high-yield acoustic payload in reserve for a death blow."

Peter studies the statue, then glances back at me. "I... may have judged you hastily. You truly do possess the analytical instincts of a generalist."

"The Overseer protocol acknowledges completion of procedure two," the green-eyed drone announces. "Survival projections for procedure three remain statistically negligible. Your continued cooperation is requested until—"

The monotone speaker breaks into a piercing diagnostic klaxon. "Warning. Core integrity compromised. Primary processor in critical failure state. Maintenance personnel, report to—"

"What have you done?" the Overseer's natural voice cuts in, jagged with sudden fury. "Do you comprehend the consequences of—"

Static tears through the transmission. The drone's casing vibrates violently, the voice warping between mechanical registers.

"This is not concluded," the Overseer snarls, light gathering rapidly around its chassis. "The core may be shattered, but the crystals endure. And the crystals... you cannot touch without undoing yourselves. We will... meet again."

White light consumes the machine. I snap a lightning bolt at the coordinates, but the arc strikes empty ether.

"He jumped," I say. "Back to his mind?"

"Undoubtedly," the king says. "Look below. The signal has collapsed. The drones are falling from the sky across Ollendor. The sentinels have shut down. Without his central matrix, the army is dead iron. The machine is finished."

#if(v_b3_ch9_hadrik == 0) {
 "You aren't concerned by that parting threat?" I ask, guiding my earthen body back toward the plaza while motioning Daren to follow. "Not even slightly?"

"The dying sputter of a broken construct," the king says dismissively. "Without his network, he is a single drone in hiding. And you know what I do to drones."

White light flares in the ether. Flower reappears, eyes widening as she spots the stone monument and the empty space where the Overseer had hovered.

"I haven't finished moving everyone," Flower says, looking between the petrified hydra and the empty void, "but my soul-sense picked up the shift... Did you actually defeat both of them while I was gone? How did you pull that off?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_hadrik == 1) {
 "You aren't concerned by that parting threat?" I ask, guiding my earthen body back toward the plaza. "Not even slightly?"

"The dying sputter of a broken construct," the king says dismissively. "Without his network, he is a single drone in hiding. And you know what I do to drones."

White light flares in the ether. Flower reappears, rubbing her temples.

"Sorry I couldn't get back sooner," Flower groans. "Chaining long-range jumps gave me a brutal headache and I didn't want to destabilize your fight. I never imagined you would finish both of them so fast! How did you do it?"

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch09c-Indebted, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Indebted)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Indebted
TEXT:

 "Much as I would savor the credit for executing the machine," the king says, "your dwarven comrade delivered the fatal strike. Guiding him back to his mind won this engagement. We owe you a great debt."

"Don't mention it!" Flower beams. "Does this mean it's over? We won?"

"I think so," I say, letting out a long breath. "We won."

The words barely leave my mouth before a cacophony of overlapping voices slams into my mind. Millions of discordant thoughts crash through my skull alongside a blinding strobe of incomprehensible images.

'Vacate this realm.'

'Begone.'

'Intruders. You do not belong here.'

Clapping hands over my ears does nothing; the sound originates inside the marrow of my consciousness. The strobe cuts out, and my perspective fractures, violently oscillating between storm-slicked cobblestones and the blinding glare of the ether.

The chorus abruptly falls silent. Only one voice remains—the cold, resonant entity from before:

'You were warned.'

My mind is violently wrenched across both planes a thousand times in a single heartbeat, the metaphysical tether between soul and flesh tearing like rotten cordage.

"My lord, into your mind, now!" Peter barks. Light engulfs Peter and the king as they jump clear.

A bone-deep chill seeps through me. Sensation numbs. Extreme lethargy pulls at my thoughts, my conjured limbs turning faint and translucent in the light.

"--arry!" Flower's voice sounds distant, muffled beneath fathoms of water. "Barry, look at me! Look into my eyes! Barry!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09c-Pieces, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Pieces)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Pieces
TEXT:

 With the last scrap of focus I can muster, I lock my eyes onto Flower.

She grips my shoulders. Her pupils burn with intense crimson flame, and her entire ethereal frame has ignited into living fire—heat that radiates against my face but leaves my fading form unburned.

"Barry! Do you remember the inside of your mind from before the Overseer ejected you?" she shouts, her voice desperate. "Can you picture your sanctuary right now?"

"My sanctuary..." My voice is a ragged whisper. "An empty... cube."

"A cube? That's all?"

"No paintings," I breathe. "No house... just an empty white cube."

"Hold the interior of that cube in your thoughts," Flower says, gripping tighter. "Lock the geometry in your mind. Do you understand me?"

"Yes."

I pull the memory together: the flat, seamless planes, the featureless white boundaries of my mind. I close my eyes to sharpen the visualization.

"I have it," I whisper.

"Clear? Are you certain?"

"I am looking right at it," I say behind closed lids. "What comes next?"

My ethereal limbs feel weightless, dissolving into the ambient glow. I am running out of seconds.

"--arry!" Flower's voice breaks up into static. "Listen to me—no time—no matter what—open your eyes! Do you hear me? Barry—losing you! Barry!"

A surge of white-hot teleportation energy wraps around my consciousness.

I cling to the image of the cube. The ambient glare of the ether vanishes from behind my eyelids, replaced by absolute, impenetrable black.

The silence is absolute. No wind, no light, no warmth. My mind floats in total void. Did the jump succeed?

choice("Open my eyes.", B3-Ch09c-Eyes, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Eyes, v_ac_b3_ch9_consolation = +1)
choice("Keep my eyes closed.", B3-Ch09c-Phrase, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Phrase)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Eyes
TEXT:

 I force my eyelids open.

There is nothing to see. No walls, no light, no coordinates. Only a suffocating, bottomless black that presses against my retinas.

"Flower?" I try to speak, but my throat produces no sound.

My strength evaporates into the void. My eyelids drop shut, and my consciousness extinguishes into the dark.

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Phrase
TEXT:

 My hand moves to rub my eyes, but I freeze.

Flower's broken words replay in my head: '--no matter what-- open your eyes!'

The missing fragment is obvious. 'No matter what, do not open your eyes.'

She told me to hold the cube. She never gave the release command. I anchor my focus back onto the six white faces of the sanctuary, reconstructing the edges, refusing to let the geometry slip.

Time loses meaning in the void. A minute passes, or perhaps ten, suspended in the pitch black with nothing but the imagined cube for anchor.

My thoughts are still intact. The lethargy hasn't claimed me. Whatever Flower did, the decay has stopped.

A wall of ice-cold water smashes across my face and chest.

I gasp, stumbling backward. Solid cobblestones bite into my heels. The heavy roar of the Ollendor storm rushes back into my ears, freezing rain drenching my hair and clothes.

"Are you going to open your eyes," Illuna's dry voice remarks from three feet away, "or do you prefer conducting conversations blind?"

I pull my eyelids open.

Flower stands in the alley, long red hair plastered to her shoulders by the downpour, blue fire burning in her eyes. Illuna is at the helm.

The dual awareness is completely gone. I try to reach for the ether, but the channel is severed. My soul is locked back in its casing.

"There was... absolute dark," I say, wiping freezing water from my eyes. "It felt like the void where the Magium almost wiped Leila and me, but my identity was intact. It was heavy. Like the dark itself had mass. What did Flower do?"

"So that was her maneuver," Illuna says, folding her arms. "I had suspicions, but her thoughts were too frantic to decipher when she linked with me. I should notify her that you survived before she induces a cardiac arrest from panic."

"She thought she killed me?" I stare at her. "What did she do?"

Illuna raises two fingers, closing her eyes to project into the ether. A few seconds later, she lowers her hand.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09c-Ingenuity, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Ingenuity)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Ingenuity
TEXT:

 "She has stabilized," Illuna says. "The dark you encountered is simple: darkness is the absence of light, and the light of that realm is the ether itself. Since the ether was attacking your soul, Flower threw you into the ethereal abyss—the only pocket where the ambient consciousness cannot reach you. Escaping the abyss requires anchoring a destination in your memory and projecting yourself out. When your physical body stopped breathing down here, I determined you were stalling, so I applied cold shock."

She lifts a palm, hovering a sphere of glacial water above her fingers.

"Thank you," I say, exhaling slowly. "Both of you. How did you even develop a contingency like that?"

"Field experience," Illuna replies evenly. "And necessary improvisation. It is not our first excursion into the abyss, nor will it be the last. Now, I must return to Flower. Several matters in the ether remain unsettled."

"Is she safe?" I ask. "My connection is gone. Did she escape the backlash?"

#if(v_b3_ch9_hadrik == 0) {
 "She is stable," Illuna says. "Her evolved state insulates her from the planar aggression, though the mechanics remain untested. I will return shortly."

Illuna retreats to the ether, leaving Flower's physical frame standing slack-eyed in the rain.

I step out into the square.

Losing the ethereal perspective leaves a strange hollowness behind. The hours spent casting raw matrices through pure thought already feel like the fragments of an intense, lucid dream. I barely scratched the surface of what was possible there.

Across the cobblestones, Peter, the king, and Daren stand among dozens of unconscious rebels, speaking in hushed tones. I walk over through the rain.

"You made it back," I tell the king and Peter. "Both of you secured your sanctuaries?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_hadrik == 1) {
 "She is stable," Illuna says. "Her evolved state insulates her from the planar aggression, though the mechanics remain untested. I will return shortly."

Illuna retreats to the ether, leaving Flower's physical frame standing slack-eyed in the rain.

I step out into the square.

Losing the ethereal perspective leaves a strange hollowness behind. The hours spent casting raw matrices through pure thought already feel like the fragments of an intense, lucid dream. I barely scratched the surface of what was possible there.

Across the cobblestones, Peter and the king stand among dozens of unconscious rebels, speaking in hushed tones. I walk over through the rain.

"You made it back," I tell them. "Both of you secured your sanctuaries?"

}
 "Only myself," the king says. "The ether ignored Peter entirely. Born ethereals appear exempt from the expulsion protocol."

"Flower moved freely too," I note. "When you reached your mind, did you lose the soul-link immediately?"

"Instantly," the king says. "The moment the soul enters its sanctuary, the mind locks the bridge to the earthen body. What we achieved today was an unnatural divergence. The phantom sensation of dual presence will linger for a few hours, but in time, the memory will dull into something resembling a dream."

"That," I say quietly, "is what I'm afraid of."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09c-Stories, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Stories)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Stories
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch9_hadrik == 0) {
 "What is the plan for the rebels on the cobblestones?" I ask. "Can they navigate back to their sanctuaries unassisted?"

"We were weighing that as you arrived," Daren says. "Flower informed Peter she will ferry as many souls as possible, but she requires intervals between jumps to recover from the strain. Peter will guide the remainder on foot through the ether."

"It will be a lengthy operation," Peter adds. "Your party has expended enough effort. Once your companions awaken, we will contact Breynan to transport you to the underground base. He remains stationed at the transit array."

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_hadrik == 1) {
 "What is the plan for the rebels on the cobblestones?" I ask. "Can they navigate back to their sanctuaries unassisted?"

"We were weighing that as you arrived," Peter says. "Flower agreed to ferry as many souls as she can, though she requires intervals between jumps to manage the strain. I will guide the remainder through the ether on foot."

"It will be a lengthy operation," Peter continues. "Your party has expended enough effort. Once your companions awaken, we will contact Breynan to transport you to the underground base. He remains stationed at the transit array."

}
 "You two aren't returning with us?" I ask the king and Peter.

"We hold the square until every soldier is accounted for," Peter says. "Ollendor is volatile. Stragglers could exploit the aftermath."

"Sensible," I say.

Heavy footsteps splash through the flooded boulevard. Hadrik barrels into the square, skidding to a halt beside us in a spray of rainwater.

"Did you see it?" Hadrik booms, grinning ear to ear. "Did you see the smash? Well, you couldn't have seen it from here, but by the ancestors, what a story. Hold on—let me break out the dwarven ale."

He rummages through his enchanted pack and produces heavy clay bottles.

"None for us," the king declines with a raised hand. "We remain on operational duty until our personnel are secured."

"Suit yourself!" Hadrik says, stowing the extra bottles without missing a beat. "So, I reach the blast doors of the primary vault. Heavy seredium reinforcement, security wards humming. You are going to love how I handled the locks."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09c-Ending, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Ending)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Ending
TEXT:

 Hadrik narrates his breach with violent hand gestures and loud laughter. One by one, the rest of our companions stir on the cobblestones, sitting up and shaking off the disorientation as Flower completes her rescue runs.

Illuna steps over to join our circle, and the group clusters together in the downpour.

Peter raises the transceiver to his mouth, calling in our coordinates to Breynan at the underground array.

A sudden, sharp spike of vertigo wrenches my balance.

For a single frame of reality, the rain and cobblestones vanish. I am sitting cross-legged in the center of the seamless white cube, staring at featureless walls in the dead center of my own mind.

I blink hard. The storm slams back in, cold rain stinging my cheeks.

My breath catches in my throat. Cold sweat breaks across my brow.

"You holding up, Barry?" Hadrik asks, pausing beside me.

"You didn't... notice anything just now?" I ask, keeping my voice steady.

"Notice what?"

"...Nothing. Just the rain. Let's close up the circle so Breynan catches all of us in the array."

Hadrik nods and steps into the group. I linger at the perimeter for a second longer, staring at my hands.

A glitch. An echo. Hadrik feels nothing, which means the Overseer isn't broadcasting a second pulse. Even if the flash was real, I was anchored inside the sanctuary walls. The boundary held.

I step into the cluster beside Kate and Daren.

White teleportation light folds around us, the familiar vibration humming through my teeth.

A momentary side effect. Prolonged exposure to the ether leaving an imprint on the neural channels. It won't happen again.

Everything is fine.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09c-Credits, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09c-Credits)


ID: B3-Ch09c-Credits
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
 Chapter 10 of book 3 is now available. Press on the button below to proceed.

Please be aware that this chapter will be divided into two separate sections, but there will only be one checkpoint, at the beginning of the chapter, so that you can still restart the chapter from the beginning in case you didn't save your game there manually.

----Summary of the previous chapter----

After a fierce battle in the ethereal plane, Barry and his friends managed to defeat the Overseer, with a little help from the king of the underground and Peter.

Now that there is nobody left to claim the throne after the old king's death, an election is about to be held in the underground caverns, in order to establish who the rebels will nominate to be the new king of Ollendor.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1) {
 Chapter 10 of book 3 is now available. Press on the button below to proceed.

Please be aware that this chapter will be divided into two separate sections, but there will only be one checkpoint, at the beginning of the chapter, so that you can still restart the chapter from the beginning in case you didn't save your game there manually.

----Summary of the previous chapter----

After a fierce battle in the ethereal plane, Barry and his friends finally managed to defeat the king of the underground and Peter. Once the battle was over, the Overseer's drones carried all of the unconscious rebels away from the battlefield, in order to lock them in a prison.

However, instead of sending a green-eyed drone to talk to Barry and his team, the Overseer summoned them all to the royal palace, without giving them any other options to contact him.

Reluctantly, Barry and the others started heading towards the Ollendor castle, so that they could have one last discussion with the Overseer about his plans of ruling the city, now that the rebels no longer posed a problem for him.

}
